<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Shelley returns two weeks after presenting with headaches and spells of numbness and weakness of the right hand and leg. The MRI eleven days ago shows a 3.1 cm dural-based mass on the left frontoparietal convexity, over the part of the motor cortex that controls the right hand, with a dural tail and moderate swelling of the surrounding brain; its appearance is typical of a meningioma, and there is no stroke. The EEG a week ago shows sharp waves and slowing over the left central region. She had one more spell five days ago: tingling that climbed from her right fingers to the elbow, with twitching of the thumb, for about a minute, fully aware throughout. She has stopped driving. She saw Dr. Ferreira-Lindgren of neurosurgery this morning, looked at the images with him, and asked many questions. She was relieved that the spells have a cause that can be seen, and frightened by the word tumor. She has had no fall, confusion, or vomiting, and the morning headaches are unchanged. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MRI</td><td>3.1 cm left convexity meningioma over the hand motor area, moderate edema, 2 mm shift</td></tr><tr><td>EEG</td><td>Left central sharp waves and slowing</td></tr><tr><td>Interval change</td><td>One further focal aware seizure of the right hand</td></tr><tr><td>Driving</td><td>Stopped since the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Neurosurgery</td><td>Consultation this morning</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

