  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Left frontoparietal convexity meningioma with vasogenic edema (D32.0)</content>. A symptomatic meningioma with edema, causing seizures and mild right-hand weakness; the presenting concern is resolved into this diagnosis and focal epilepsy. Observation is not advised for a symptomatic tumor with edema. Stereotactic radiosurgery is less suitable for a 3.1 cm tumor with edema next to the motor cortex, and gives no tissue diagnosis. Resection aiming at removal of the tumor with its dural attachment (Simpson grade I) is likely to be curative and to relieve the seizures and weakness. She chose resection after hearing the three options with their benefits and risks. [S]<list><item>Left craniotomy and resection in three weeks with Dr. Ferreira-Lindgren, with neuronavigation and motor mapping.</item><item>Dexamethasone 4 mg twice a day from three days before surgery, then tapered after it; not started now, because the weakness is mild.</item><item>MRI within 48 hours after surgery; pathology with WHO CNS5 grading.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Focal epilepsy due to the meningioma (G40.109)</content>. Recurrent focal aware seizures with a structural cause and an epileptiform EEG meet the definition of epilepsy, with a high risk of recurrence, so an antiseizure medicine is indicated now rather than prophylaxis. [S]<list><item>Levetiracetam 500 mg twice a day from today; it can cause irritability or low mood, so tell the clinic if either appears.</item><item>Keep a seizure diary; no driving until seizures are controlled for the period her state requires and the neurosurgeon agrees.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the brain with gadolinium at presentation</td><td>At presentation with focal symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Eleven days ago: 3.1 x 2.7 x 2.6 cm left convexity meningioma, moderate edema, 2 mm shift; MR angiography normal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>EEG</td><td>After a suspected seizure</td><td>Up to date</td><td>A week ago: left central sharp waves and slowing</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurosurgery consultation</td><td>At diagnosis of a symptomatic meningioma</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen this morning; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Somatostatin-receptor PET or other imaging for diagnostic doubt</td><td>Only if the diagnosis is uncertain</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Typical appearance on MRI; tissue will be obtained at surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine for seizures, not prophylaxis</td><td>Start after seizures; no prophylaxis in seizure-free patients</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Levetiracetam started for recurrent focal seizures; kidney function normal two weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Corticosteroid for edema only if needed</td><td>For symptomatic edema, at the lowest dose and for the shortest time</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Mild deficit; a short course is planned around surgery only</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count and clotting before craniotomy</td><td>Before surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 12.7 g/dL, platelets 248, INR 1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure diary, triggers (sleep loss, alcohol, missed doses), and seizure first aid</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One focal aware seizure five days ago; diary started; first aid reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving and safety advice</td><td>From the first suspected seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; showers only; no swimming alone</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9, with a question about irritability on levetiracetam</td><td>Every visit while on levetiracetam and during cancer treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>8, mild, in the week of the diagnosis; no irritability yet; the neuro-oncology counselor offered and accepted</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>Every visit during the diagnostic and treatment phase</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not repeated today; see the next visit</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A diagnosis within two weeks, explained in plain words with the images and reports. Outcome by 1849-03-17: MRI and EEG within a week; diagnosis explained at two weeks with the images [S]</item><item>No further seizures. In progress; target 1850-04-07 [S]</item><item>Full use of the right hand and return to writing every morning. In progress; target 1849-09-29 [S]</item><item>Decide on treatment herself after hearing the options, benefits, and harms. Outcome by 1849-03-17: Chose resection after observation and radiosurgery were explained [S]</item><item>Mood and grief supported: PHQ-9 below 5 and a counselor available. In progress; target 1849-09-29 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EANO guideline on the diagnosis and management of meningiomas. Goldbrunner R, et al. Neuro Oncol 2021;23(11):1821-1834 (2021); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Central Nervous System Cancers, Version 2.2025 (2025); Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Krumholz A, et al. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (2015); ILAE official report: a practical clinical definition of epilepsy. Fisher RS, et al. Epilepsia 2014;55(4):475-482 (2014); SNO and EANO practice guideline update: Anticonvulsant prophylaxis in patients with newly diagnosed brain tumors. Walbert T, et al. Neuro Oncol 2021;23(11):1835-1844 (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (depression screening and PHQ-9 follow-up) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Craniotomy in three weeks; primary care review three weeks after surgery; counselor appointment next week. Next scheduled visit 1849-04-28: review three weeks after craniotomy, with the pathology. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
